    "body": "## ADR-KERNEL-09 (PROPOSED): control-plane authorization through the GovernanceKernel\n\n**This is a design, not an implementation, and it is not accepted.** It is the owner's Nov 2 decision made concrete: control-plane governance is a mandatory follow-up with its own design, separate from PR #94, and **required before DEBT-025 can close**. **DEBT-025 stays OPEN**; this PR does not touch its status, and it deliberately does not edit `KNOWN_ISSUES.md` (PR #94 edits the same DEBT-025 row).\nDocs only: `docs/architecture/decisions/ADR_KERNEL_09_CONTROL_PLANE_AUTHORIZATION.md` plus its row in `ADR_INDEX.md`. Base `main` `2491ae4`. ADR-KERNEL numbers 01-08 are taken on `main` and on other branches; 09 is free.\n\n### The owner's requirements it is written against\nR1 every sensitive control-plane operation gets an explicit `GovernanceKernel` decision; R2 before any side effect; R3 missing policy, identity or governance fails closed; R4 decisions name actor, operation, resource and outcome; R5 tests prove unauthorized requests cannot modify configuration, modules, imports, training state or update state; R6 a local-owner principal is allowed at first but the boundary must be genuine, not a pass-through.\n\n### What I found in the code (verified at `2491ae4`, each fact has a line reference in the ADR)\n- **The kernel is open by default.** The base `Governor.evaluate` returns `APPROVE` and the kernel approves when no governor objects, so an action type no governor recognises is approved. That is the opposite of R3, and it means \"just call `evaluate_action()` from the API\" would not satisfy the requirement.\n- `GovernanceAction` has no actor and no resource field (R4).\n- The kernel does not persist its decisions; a grep of `core/governance/` finds no `EventStream` use.\n- `interface/api.py` never calls the kernel. The only production caller is `core/orchestrator.py`; drift rules (DRIFT-05, DRIFT-10) restrict other components.\n- `EventStream.append` is async and durable and **raises on failure**, so a lost audit record is detectable and can fail closed. Its default path is cwd-relative (noted, out of scope).\n- 11 control-plane operations with real side effects are inventoried with handler lines: `config.write` (rewrites `settings.toml`), `module.create`, `module.train`, `module.distill`, `brain.export`, `brain.import`, `update.install`, `update.restart` (`os.execv`), `update.rollback`, `evolve.plan`, `execute.query`.\n\n### The proposal in one paragraph\nA default-deny `ControlPlaneGovernor` for `control.*` actions, an immutable principal attached by the auth middleware, `GovernanceAction` gaining optional `actor` and `resource` fields, **one** authorization service (`core/governance/control_plane.py`) as the only place the API reaches the kernel, an `authorize()` call as the first state-relevant statement of each governed handler (before any task is scheduled), and a `control.decision` event that **must persist before approval is returned** (audit failure denies). Config can only tighten policy. A route-coverage test makes the inventory enforceable: a new protected route cannot ship without a tier.\n\n### What is not claimed\n- Nothing is implemented; the 12-item test plan (§6) is a plan, not results.\n- `[UNKNOWN]` and flagged in the ADR: what `upgrade_planner.propose_upgrades` persists, and the internals of `_scheduler.trigger_module`. I did not trace them.\n- Line numbers are for `main` `2491ae4` and shift once PR #94 merges.\n\n### Decisions left to the owner (§9)\n- **O1 opt-out principal:** with auth disabled there is no credential. Recommendation: allow T1/T2 but deny T3 (config, code, version), so an unauthenticated API cannot reconfigure, import code or update.\n- **O2 reads and `execute.query`:** authentication-only for reads (covered by the coverage test); authorize `/query` at the HTTP entry in addition to the existing downstream governance.\n- **O3 T3 initial verdict:** allow with audit for the local owner (there is no approval channel), versus `ESCALATE` (which today means deny).\n- **O4:** `actor`/`resource` as fields (recommended) or metadata.\n- **O5:** a new drift rule forbidding `interface/` from calling the kernel directly.\n\n### Verification\nDocs-only branch, `CI=true`: full suite 2184 passed, 1 xfailed, 0 failed (identical to `main`); `scripts/check_drift.py` no FAIL. That shows the ADR index row and file break nothing; it says nothing about the design's correctness, which is the owner's to judge.\n",
