    "pull_request": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/1h0lde4/ocbrain-v4.1/pulls/79",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/1h0lde4/ocbrain-v4.1/pull/79",
      "diff_url": "https://github.com/1h0lde4/ocbrain-v4.1/pull/79.diff",
      "patch_url": "https://github.com/1h0lde4/ocbrain-v4.1/pull/79.patch",
      "merged_at": null
    },
    "body": "## What\n`PrivacyGuard.wipe_module_data()` now builds its deletion targets with `module_child()` instead of joining `module_name` onto a path. Also replaces the only test of it, which did not exercise it.\n\n## Why\nIt ran `shutil.rmtree` on `ROOT/data/{raw,chunks}/<module_name>` with `module_name` used as a raw path component: no validation, no containment. A traversal name (`../../x`) or an **absolute** path (pathlib: `Path(\"a\") / \"/abs\" == Path(\"/abs\")`, so it replaces the root) could aim `rmtree` outside the data directories. Same class as CTX-EXPORT-001, and the one site that bypassed the shared `core/module_paths.module_child()` helper when `acc43b7` routed \"every module-name filesystem path\" through it. CodeQL never flagged it — consistent with it having no reachable caller.\n\n## Reachability: latent, not live\n`git grep` finds **no production caller** of `wipe_module_data`/`wipe_all`, and the old `test_wipe_module_data` replaced `PrivacyGuard` with a stub and then called `shutil.rmtree` itself — it tested the standard library and would pass against any implementation. So this closes a sink **before** anything wires it to an endpoint; it does not fix a currently-exploitable hole.\n\n## Evidence (exploit-first)\n- Tests commit run against the **unfixed** code: **8 failed, 6 passed.** I checked the failures are for the right reason: the decoy directory outside `data/` was actually deleted (`decoy.is_dir()` -> `False`), for both the traversal and the absolute-path payload; no invalid name raised.\n- After the fix: **14 passed.** Full suite **2012 passed, 1 xfailed**; `test_module_name_containment.py` 76/76; `check_drift.py` 15/15.\n\n## Test-design note (worth a reviewer's eye)\nA red run of a deletion-exploit test *executes the exploit*. Every payload therefore resolves inside `tmp_path` (the fake repo root is injected via `core.privacy.__file__`). An early draft listed a bare `\"/etc\"` as a payload; against unfixed code that is `rmtree(\"/etc\")`. The rule is written into the test file's header.\n\n## Behaviour change\nAn invalid `module_name` now raises `InvalidModuleName` (a `ValueError`) before anything is deleted, instead of attempting the deletion. No production caller exists, so no compatibility impact. A module with no data is still a no-op (`module_child` tolerates a missing child).\n\n## Not covered / limits\n- This is **one** sink. A systematic sweep for other unguarded destructive sinks (R7) is **still open**; this PR does not claim \"every path is guarded\".\n- Line endings: `core/privacy.py` and `tests/test_privacy.py` are CRLF and are preserved (diffstat 6+/1- for the source file).\n- CI has not run yet on this branch at the time of writing.\n",
    "closed_by": null,
